    kind: 'move', n: 9, id: 'suspense', title: 'Keep Them in Suspense: What Comes Next?', phase: 'astray',
    line: 'Once they can predict you, they stop watching you. Keep the next move hidden, and they never look away.',
    gist: 'Greene’s rule is that predictability kills desire. The moment a target feels they know what you will say and do next, the charge goes out of things: you have become part of the furniture, and their attention drifts elsewhere. So the seducer plans surprises and makes them look spontaneous: an abrupt change of plan, a gift for no reason, a sudden trip, a mood or a side of themselves the other has never seen. Each jolt knocks the target off balance, opens them up and keeps their thoughts fixed on you, wondering what comes next. It also hands you the lead, because someone who cannot anticipate you can only follow. The surprises must vary, or the pattern itself becomes predictable.',
