      text: 'A researcher approached people about to use a university photocopier and asked to go first with five pages. With no reason given, 60 per cent agreed. With a real one, “because I’m in a rush”, 94 per cent did. With an empty one, “because I have to make copies”, which was true of everyone there, 93 per cent did. The word “because” did the work of a reason. Only when the request grew to twenty pages did people start listening to what the reason actually said.',
